{:edb/db-before-t 2243 :edb/db-after-t 2244 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535556 50 #inst "2026-10-09T02:27:21.010Z" 13194139535556 true] [17592186064174 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355063 13194139535556 true] [17592186064175 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355067 13194139535556 true] [17592186064872 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535556 true] [17592186064872 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t) = 2e^{2t}$, where $t \\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x = e^{2}$ and velocity $v = e^{2}$ when $t = 1$, calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t > 0$." 13194139535556 false] [17592186064872 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=2{e}^{2t}$, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x={e}^{2}$ and velocity $v={e}^{2}$ when $t=1$, calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t>0.$" 13194139535556 true] [17592186064872 1145 "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 2{e}^{2t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)={e}^{2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2} & ={e}^{2(1)}+C \\\\ {e}^{2} & ={e}^{2}+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)={e}^{2t}.$ \n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{2t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K,$ we use the fact that $x(1)={e}^{2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2(1)}+K \\\\ {e}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}+K \\\\ K & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139535556 true] [17592186289955 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355066 13194139535556 true] [17592186355063 1017 #edb/ref 17592186355064 13194139535556 true] [17592186355063 1089 "q-351554" 13194139535556 true] [17592186355063 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535556 true] [17592186355063 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=-7,$ where $t>0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=30$ when $t=4,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$\n\n$x(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535556 true] [17592186355063 1113 #uuid "c66c33a8-1671-5b74-a094-92b58002d291" 13194139535556 true] [17592186355063 1145 "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int -7\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-7t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $x(4)=30.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30 & =-7(4)+C \\\\ 30 & =-28+C \\\\ C & =58.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=-7t+58.\n$$" 13194139535556 true] [17592186355064 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355065 13194139535556 true] [17592186355064 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535556 true] [17592186355064 1041 "field-1" 13194139535556 true] [17592186355064 1104 #edb/ref 17592186355065 13194139535556 true] [17592186355064 1110 #uuid "95e093ee-f3aa-5a6f-91aa-e54b066389f5" 13194139535556 true] [17592186355065 1034 "-7t+58" 13194139535556 true] [17592186355065 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535556 true] [17592186355065 1111 #uuid "b7a48b8d-9bde-546e-bb8f-b07e755fe2db" 13194139535556 true] [17592186355066 1034 "1+\\cot(t)" 13194139535556 true] [17592186355066 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535556 true] [17592186355066 1111 #uuid "a4cd12a8-3168-55fa-b6c1-5969ee91eba0" 13194139535556 true] [17592186355067 1017 #edb/ref 17592186355068 13194139535556 true] [17592186355067 1089 "q-351732" 13194139535556 true] [17592186355067 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535556 true] [17592186355067 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)={e}^{-2t},$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=0$ and velocity $v=0$ when $t=0,$ the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$x(t)=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535556 true] [17592186355067 1113 #uuid "d60b6719-04f5-5e7d-a166-15c23dff2e1b" 13194139535556 true] [17592186355067 1145 "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2t}}{-2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =\\frac{1}{-2}{e}^{0}+C \\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{1}{2}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2t}+\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2t}+\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-2t}+\\frac{1}{2}t+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K$, we use the fact that $x(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{0}+\\frac{1}{2}(0)+K \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{1}{4}+K \\\\ K & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-2t}+\\frac{1}{2}t-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$" 13194139535556 true] [17592186355068 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355069 13194139535556 true] [17592186355068 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535556 true] [17592186355068 1041 "field-1" 13194139535556 true] [17592186355068 1104 #edb/ref 17592186355069 13194139535556 true] [175921